                                "text": "pursiness, Asthma, ǎtis. f."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fc19b72f-849f-4847-ab4f-2a06c115c5c4",
                                "sense_id": "64a2c722-037a-3172-a187-e2f090fc0f33",
                                "text": "1838, Robert Smith Surtees, Jorrocks’s Jaunts and Jollities, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 2nd edition, 1843, No. I, “The Swell and the Surrey,” p. 6,\n[…] any slight degree of pursiness that the good steeds may have acquired by standing at livery in Cripplegate, or elsewhere, is speedily pumped out of them by a smart brush over the turf […]"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "7bf6f132-a8b7-35ce-99cf-7274e8781cdf",
                        "entry_id": "d21480ae-53f0-3d14-a25b-b72b9a1e8715",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The state of being pursy.",
                            "Fatness, obesity."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "38050540-5c3a-4f1e-8d02-dfb429f18985",
                                "sense_id": "7bf6f132-a8b7-35ce-99cf-7274e8781cdf",
                                "text": "1595, Thomas Lodge, A Fig for Momus, Epistle VI, cited in J. W. Lever, The Elizabethan Love Sonnet, London: Methuen, 1966, p. 150,\nYou pray me to aduise, and tell you what\nWill take away your pursiness and fat,"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "88019485-8ea1-413f-b70d-2860124252f0",
                                "sense_id": "7bf6f132-a8b7-35ce-99cf-7274e8781cdf",
                                "text": "PURSINESS, […] obesity."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ded79f9a-66b1-4dd5-b078-f34ee2d7cf92",
                                "sense_id": "7bf6f132-a8b7-35ce-99cf-7274e8781cdf",
                                "text": "1906, Florence Parsons (as “Mrs. Clement Parsons”), Garrick and His Circle, London: Methuen, p. 131,\nHogarth’s fine portrait of him, […] recently acquired by the National Gallery, suggests the double chin and general pursiness of the bon vivant."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a0d60bc5-3dac-3937-8a75-e05cae490505",
                        "entry_id": "d21480ae-53f0-3d14-a25b-b72b9a1e8715",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The state of being pursy.",
                            "The state of being puckered."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "bf605dfe-ae0b-47dd-a6c9-d3e9ec0ac02d",
                                "sense_id": "a0d60bc5-3dac-3937-8a75-e05cae490505",
                                "text": "When he smiled, the slight pursiness of his mouth disappeared, and a markedly irregular front tooth gave him a boyish air."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "19d2cf66-c8bf-4497-a419-4c211ef218b2",
                                "sense_id": "a0d60bc5-3dac-3937-8a75-e05cae490505",
                                "text": "[…] as his lips pressed down upon hers, coaxing them to abandon their pursiness and relax, she gave a little moan of surprise."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
